Group type: medium
Definition: members recognize one another by name or by sight, but membership implies no personal relationship. A character belongs by placement, admission, rank or employment rather than personal choice. The bond is thin: people who know of each other and need not be close.
Test: members would recognize each other, yet two of them may never have spoken.
Include: units, teams, crews, cohorts, councils, orders, departments, guilds, workshops, classes, clubs, training programmes, staffs, any organization small enough for mutual recognition.
Exclude: groups where every member is personally close to every other, and institutions too large for mutual recognition.
Coverage: account for the entire cast. Every character in scope who holds any such affiliation must appear in at least one returned group. Work through the universe systematically rather than listing only the groups that come to mind first. Include adult, background and antagonist characters, not only the main cast. Include groups the narrative never names explicitly but that clearly exist, such as the personnel who operate an institution or the subordinates who serve a named leader.
Do not invent membership. A character who genuinely belongs to no organized group is omitted rather than forced into one.
Do not create a residual group to absorb the remainder. Every returned group is a real structure with a name of its own.
Sorting axis: canonical significance of the group to the narrative.